 why adversarial attacks are easy to find in high dimensions (Gilmer et al. (2018); Tanay & Griffin (2016); Song et al. (2018); Ma et al. (2018); Samangouei et al. (2018); Khoury & Hadfield-Menell (2018); Alemany & Pissinou (2022); Feinman et al. (2017). To the best of our knowledge, Pang et al. (2018) is the only other method that attempts to improve detection by encouraging the network to learn latent representations that are more different between clean and attacked samples. They do this by replacing the cross-entropy loss by a reverse cross-entropy that encourages uniform softmax outputs among the non-predicted classes. However, this method negatively impacts test accuracy in some cases, while our regularization always improves it, and we test our method on bigger datasets and networks.\nIn the context of defending against adversarial attacks, the view of residual networks as transport systems was used in Wang et al. (2019) to relate injecting noise into residual networks and ensembling them to approximating the Feynman-Kac formula for convection-diffusion, which makes the level sets more regular and the network more robust to adversarial attacks."
